  "body": "Found in the second safety read of PR #3126 (comment 7073890540). These notes do not block that PR, and they give no wrong output.\n\n1. After PR #3126, a drafted loop counts as sharp when other loops of the same operation carry a modelled chamfer. Take a plate whose outer sides all have a draft of 3 degrees or more:\n   - with a modelled-chamfer island, the drafted outline is no longer broken (`main` broke it before);\n   - with a modelled-chamfer pocket inside it, the operation now refuses with \"the edge around it is sharp\" (`main` rode both loops before).\n   Either give the refusal a message that names the drafted side, or record the choice in `docs/decisions/2929-chamfer-drafted-side.md`.\n2. The code does not check the sign of the draft. For an undercut (a negative draft), the top of the chamfer comes out as `w(1 + tan(draft))` instead of `w(1 - tan(draft))`. `main` behaves the same way for a loop that is offset all round. Add a test, and refuse or handle the undercut.\n3. A tilted frame has no test.\n\nThis is under the safety floor (chamfer geometry), so the effort is `effort:high`.\n",
